30,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"My findings suggest that catalogue-style projects provide lasting value, whereas those chasing specific mechanisms often leave their core questions unanswered.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"We have a personnel crisis. Most phenotyping technicians have left for industry, though we have plenty of applicants for modelling and code posts.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The infrastructure is strained. The spectroscopy suite was idle for eleven weeks, and the glasshouses are completely booked for two more cycles.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems the agency still subsidizes glasshouse space, but shipping costs are rising while per-diems remain frozen. Computing, however, is now free.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I found that projects relying on live breeding colonies are far more prone to delays, and several field sites were lost to fire and flooding.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Ryuji, the lack of technicians is concerning. If we choose a project requiring heavy phenotyping, it may stall regardless of its scientific promise.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shizue, exactly. We must avoid any proposal that depends on specialized technical hands that are no longer in the building.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the free computing is a relief, but it doesn't help if the physical equipment, like the spectroscopy suite, is broken or unsupported.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, a fair point. It appears we are moving toward a digital paradise while the physical world remains stubbornly fragmented and expensive.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, the glasshouse shortage is a huge red flag. Any project needing those spaces right now will be delayed before it even starts.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Shizue,,We must prioritize a project that is robust. A catalogue-style approach seems safer and more enduring than a mechanism-focused one.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,I agree. We need a project that aligns with our current staffing—more modelling and less manual phenotyping.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Victoria,,We should avoid anything requiring the glasshouses or the spectroscopy suite. The risk of systemic failure is too high.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,I concur. We should favor projects that leverage the free computing and avoid the rising costs of shipping and animal units.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Midori,,And we must avoid breeding colonies and volatile field sites. Stability is key to ensuring the researchers aren't overwhelmed.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
