55,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Looking at the candidates, the Microbiome Atlas is a catalogue project. It avoids the mechanism-focused pitfalls I discovered in the archives.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The Atlas is ideal. It relies on sequencing and metadata, fitting our abundance of code-focused applicants rather than scarce phenotyping technicians.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Victoria,,It also avoids the glasshouses and the spectroscopy suite. It is the only candidate that doesn't clash with our current facility bottlenecks.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I agree. It leverages the national repository and computing, avoiding the shipping and animal-unit costs that would drain the budget.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Midori,,It's the safest choice! It doesn't need breeding colonies or the volatile field sites that caused so many previous overruns.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"The other options—Plant Drought, Amphibians, and Protein Folding—all seem to rely on the very frictions we have identified.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, the Protein Folding study would likely need that spectroscopy suite, which we know is unreliable. It's too risky.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, absolutely. And the Plant Drought project would be impossible with the glasshouses booked solid.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,The Amphibian study would be hit by the sharp rise in animal-unit rates and the volatility of breeding colonies.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Midori,,I feel very comfortable with the Atlas. It supports the researchers by removing the obstacles that usually cause stress and delays.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Shizue,,I believe we have reached a rare and beautiful consensus. The Atlas is the most sustainable and promising path.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,I am in full agreement. It is the only logical choice given the current staffing and facility constraints.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I concur. It minimizes operational risk and maximizes the chance of actual delivery.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,It is an elegant solution. I am ready to vote for the Microbiome Atlas.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Midori,,Me too! I'm so glad we found a project that can actually succeed. I vote for the Microbiome Atlas.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I cast my vote for the Microbiome Atlas.,"{""words"":8,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I vote for the Microbiome Atlas.,"{""words"":6,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
